gfx803 (Polaris, GCN3): give gfx8xx an ISA family so that triton's AMD backend
accepts the target, and give that family its real wave size.

Applies to: triton-lang/triton 0a6e13e6e31f18fa75a17ac72dee0dd1bcb507b8
(release/3.9.x).

WHY
---
Triton's AMD backend rejects every arch whose ISA family is Unknown.
TritonGPUToLLVM.cpp stops with "unsupported target", and ConvertWarpPipeline.cpp
and Membar.cpp refuse the module the same way. `computeISAFamily()` in
TargetFeatures.cpp parses the gfx number and names a family only for gfx906 and
newer. gfx803 falls through to Unknown, and no kernel compiles for it.

A family alone is not enough. `TargetFeatures::getWarpSize()` returns 64 only for
the families it lists and 32 for every other one. gfx8xx is wave64 only. At 32,
every kernel is laid out for half the lanes that a wave really has, and
reductions and layouts run half-dead.

WHAT
----
1. TargetFeatures.h: adds `GCN3` to the `ISAFamily` enum.
2. TargetFeatures.cpp: `computeISAFamily()` maps every gfx8xx arch (major == 8)
   to `GCN3`. Tonga, Fiji and Polaris share one feature set for everything this
   file gates on: wave64, no MFMA, no WMMA, no v_dot.
3. TargetFeatures.cpp: `getWarpSize()` returns 64 for `GCN3`.

`isCDNA()` and `isRDNA()` stay false for `GCN3`, so it gets no MFMA or WMMA
lowering. The packed v_dot cells of the FMA path need their own gate, which is
gfx803-vdot-gate.patch.

VERIFICATION
------------
NOT YET RE-VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE on triton 3.9. The last hardware run used
triton 3.8 (675c59878): on an RX 470, an add kernel and fp32 `tl.dot` GEMMs at
256, 512 and 1024 compiled, loaded and ran with exact results (maxerr 0.0).

Repro to run on this commit, on gfx803:
1. The same add kernel, checked against a CPU reference.
2. fp32 `tl.dot` GEMMs at 256x256x256, 512x512x512 and 1024x1024x1024, each
   checked against a CPU reference.
3. `torch.cuda.get_device_properties(0).gcnArchName`, which must report `gfx803`
   in the same venv.

